## TryHackMe Room: Hide and Seek

## A focus on digital forensics to find the flag by investigating boot sequences, user accounts, ssh keys, and plenty of decryption via CyberChef.

#### A note was discovered on the compromised system, taunting us. It suggests multiple persistence mechanisms have been implanted, ensuring that Cipher can return whenever he pleases. Here’s the note:

*Dear Specter,
I must say, it’s been a thrill dancing through your systems. You lock the doors; I pick the locks. You set up alarms; I waltz right past them. But today, my dear adversary, I’ve left you a little game.*

*I've sprinkled a few persistence implants across your system, like digital Easter eggs, and I’m giving you a sporting chance to find them. Each one has a clue because where’s the fun in a silent hack?*

*Time is on my side, always running like clockwork.*
*A secret handshake gets me in every time.*
*Whenever you set the stage, I make my entrance.*
*I run with the big dogs, booting up alongside the system.*
*I love welcome messages.*
*Find them all, and you might earn a little respect. Miss one, and well… let's say I’ll be back before you even realize I never left. Happy hunting, Specter. May the best ghost win.*

*- Cipher*

First things first, we boot up and get a lay of the land. The user folder contains a document titled *'for_specter.txt'*, so that'll be our starting point. Sure enough, it's just a similar note to the one left in our intro.

The puzzle here points towards routine updates or process running on a timer, some kind of TCP handshake, boot sequences, and welcome messages? So that gives us a few places to start. Sure enough as I was typing up my report, a software updater popped up prompting us to install some updates. Totally not suspicious.

### *Time is on my side, always running like clockwork.*
First, we'll check our crontab since this seems like it calls back to a scheduled event.

And a piece hidden in the crontab notes. Taking the decrypted flag, it didn't want to pull up the website as listed, so we can use the first characters plugged into [CyberChef](https://gchq.github.io/CyberChef/) and let it run it's magic to find the first part of the flag, THM{y0

### *A secret handshake gets me in every time.*
This could be a SSH reference. Browsing through the other users on the system, we can find one that had a ssh key with what looks like another sha2 encryption

And that's another piece of the puzzle u_g0t_

### *Whenever you set the stage, I make my entrance.*
This one had me stumped for a bit, but after digging around online a bit I found that stage could also be a reference to a user logging in. Which given that most of the other references to staging were already covered by the other hints, this feels like we're on the right track.

I think I'm getting the pattern, many kudos to CyberChef for making the decryption process so much easier. Third part of the flag, 3v3ryt

### *I run with the big dogs, booting up alongside the system.*
Looking into the references to a malicious program in the boot files. ls -la /etc/cron.d/ and /cron.daily/ to see what cron (our scheduling daemon) has.

Nothing seemingly out of the ordinary. We can also check the systemd directory.

And there we go, *cipher.service*. 

Now we've got the 4th part of the flag, h1ng_

### *I love welcome messages.*

And we're on the final piece of the puzzle. This screams message when you boot up, ala a MOTD.

And that's our final part of the flag! Now when we put it all together, we get:

THM{y0u_g0t_3v3ryth1ng_d0wn}

And that wraps it up!
